WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.012
Gradle's unit of work is a task.

2
00:00:05.012 --> 00:00:08.985
A task has a name, such as compileKotlin, test, or jar.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.447
It also declares its inputs: the source files, the classpath, and the compiler flags.

4
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:16.834
And its outputs.

5
00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:20.117
For compileKotlin, those are the class files.

6
00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:24.651
Tasks depend on other tasks.

7
00:00:24.651 --> 00:00:30.775
jar needs compiled classes, so it depends on classes, which depends on compileKotlin.

8
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.660
When you ask for build, Gradle collects every task it needs, and puts them in a task graph.

9
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.901
The graph is a DAG, a directed acyclic graph.

10
00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:43.003
The arrows point one way, and never loop back.

11
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:47.379
Gradle runs each task once, in an order that respects the arrows.

12
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.835
build itself does no work.

13
00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:54.677
It groups assemble, which makes the outputs, and check, which runs the tests.

14
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.794
Gradle wants the whole plan before it does any work.

15
00:00:58.794 --> 00:01:02.007
So every run goes through three phases, in this order.

16
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:03.444
Initialization.

17
00:01:03.444 --> 00:01:09.175
Gradle reads settings.gradle.kts, and decides which projects take part.

18
00:01:09.175 --> 00:01:12.323
In hello-jvm, the root project and app.

19
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:13.428
Configuration.

20
00:01:13.428 --> 00:01:16.495
Gradle runs the build scripts of those projects.

21
00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:19.371
They register tasks and set their properties.

22
00:01:19.371 --> 00:01:22.693
And Gradle builds the task graph for what you asked.

23
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:23.636
Execution.

24
00:01:23.636 --> 00:01:28.865
Gradle runs the actions of the tasks in the graph, in dependency order.

25
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:32.519
Careful here.

26
00:01:32.519 --> 00:01:36.189
A build script looks like a program that does the build.

27
00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:37.959
It only describes the work.

28
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:41.629
Code at the top level runs during configuration.

29
00:01:41.629 --> 00:01:44.153
So does code inside tasks.register.

30
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:48.512
Only the code inside doLast runs when the task executes.

31
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:50.015
Run the hello task.

32
00:01:50.015 --> 00:01:53.185
Lines one and two print before any task starts.

33
00:01:53.185 --> 00:01:56.220
Only line three comes from the task's action.

34
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:57.994
Now run help instead.

35
00:01:57.994 --> 00:01:59.628
Line two still prints.

36
00:01:59.628 --> 00:02:05.200
Top-level code is not lazy: it runs every time Gradle configures the build.

37
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:06.964
But line 2b is gone.

38
00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:12.935
tasks.register is lazy, so Gradle only sets up a task when the graph needs it.

39
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:19.406
So keep network calls, big file reads and calls to other programs out of top-level code.

40
00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:20.753
It slows every run.

41
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:23.856
Now, why is the second run fast?

42
00:02:23.856 --> 00:02:28.165
Before a task runs, Gradle fingerprints its inputs and outputs.

43
00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:32.939
It hashes their contents, the same idea as Git's hashes on Day 8.

44
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:39.744
Then it compares those hashes with the ones it saved in the project's .gradle folder after the last run.

45
00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:45.728
If nothing changed, it prints UP-TO-DATE, and skips the task.

46
00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:49.341
Change one byte in Main.kt, and its hash changes.

47
00:02:49.341 --> 00:02:54.139
The fingerprints differ, so the task runs again, and prints no label.

48
00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:56.838
Timestamps do not matter.

49
00:02:56.838 --> 00:03:01.252
Touch the file without changing it, and the task stays up to date.

50
00:03:01.252 --> 00:03:02.589
Only content counts.

51
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.797
Back to the graph.

52
00:03:04.797 --> 00:03:08.832
This time, change only MainTest.kt, and build again.

53
00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:14.949
compileKotlin and jar stay up to date, because their inputs are the main source files only.

54
00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:18.608
Only compileTestKotlin and test do real work.

55
00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:22.756
You will see four labels on task lines.

56
00:03:22.756 --> 00:03:25.796
UP-TO-DATE, NO-SOURCE, SKIPPED, and FROM-CACHE.

57
00:03:25.796 --> 00:03:28.253
No label means the task did real work.

58
00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:32.242
And a task that declares no outputs can never be up to date.

59
00:03:32.242 --> 00:03:35.200
There is nothing to compare, so it always runs.

60
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.147
One more trick.

61
00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:42.118
The configuration cache saves the result of the first two phases.

62
00:03:42.118 --> 00:03:45.407
hello-jvm turns it on in gradle.properties.

63
00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:49.971
The next run with the same scripts and tasks goes straight to execution.

64
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:56.460
In return, a running task may not reach back into project, the live build model.

65
00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.169
On a cache hit the build scripts never ran, so that model does not exist.

66
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.391
So Gradle forbids it on every run, hit or miss.

67
00:04:04.391 --> 00:04:06.427
The build fails with this error.

68
00:04:06.633 --> 00:04:12.379
The fix: read the value during configuration, and keep only the result for the action.

69
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.426
The build cache is a different store.

70
00:04:15.426 --> 00:04:22.707
When a task's outputs are already in it, Gradle copies them instead of doing the work, and the task line says FROM-CACHE.

71
00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:25.116
Day 30 shows how to set it up.

72
00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:27.113
Read the settings.

73
00:04:27.113 --> 00:04:29.735
Run the scripts to build the task graph.

74
00:04:29.735 --> 00:04:30.980
Then run the tasks.

75
00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:36.157
And any task whose inputs and outputs did not change is skipped, as UP-TO-DATE.
